  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,\\sqrt{5}\\}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $1\\,R\\,2$\n\nII. $2\\,R\\,2$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,2$",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $=$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\ne $ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1=2,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is  true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=$, we have $2=2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $\\sqrt{5}\\ne 2,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
